<pre>public abstract class <span class=3D"typeNameLabel">AbstractList&lt;E&=
gt;</span>
extends <a href=3D"https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/util/Abst=
ractCollection.html" title=3D"class in java.util">AbstractCollection</a>&lt=
;E&gt;
implements <a href=3D"https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/util/L=
ist.html" title=3D"interface in java.util">List</a>&lt;E&gt;</pre>
<div class=3D"block">This class provides a skeletal implementation of the <=
a href=3D"https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/util/List.html" ti=
tle=3D"interface in java.util"><code>List</code></a>
 interface to minimize the effort required to implement this interface
 backed by a "random access" data store (such as an array).  For sequential
 access data (such as a linked list), <a href=3D"https://docs.oracle.com/ja=
vase/8/docs/api/java/util/AbstractSequentialList.html" title=3D"class in ja=
va.util"><code>AbstractSequentialList</code></a> should
 be used in preference to this class.

 <p>To implement an unmodifiable list, the programmer needs only to extend
 this class and provide implementations for the <a href=3D"https://docs.ora=
cle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/util/AbstractList.html#get-int-"><code>get(i=
nt)</code></a> and
 <a href=3D"https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/util/List.html#s=
ize--"><code>size()</code></a> methods.

 </p><p>To implement a modifiable list, the programmer must additionally
 override the <a href=3D"https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/uti=
l/AbstractList.html#set-int-E-"><code>set(int, E)</code></a> method (which =
otherwise
 throws an <code>UnsupportedOperationException</code>).  If the list is
 variable-size the programmer must additionally override the
 <a href=3D"https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/util/AbstractLis=
t.html#add-int-E-"><code>add(int, E)</code></a> and <a href=3D"https://docs=
.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/util/AbstractList.html#remove-int-"><cod=
e>remove(int)</code></a> methods.

 </p><p>The programmer should generally provide a void (no argument) and co=
llection
 constructor, as per the recommendation in the <a href=3D"https://docs.orac=
le.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/util/Collection.html" title=3D"interface in j=
ava.util"><code>Collection</code></a> interface
 specification.

 </p><p>Unlike the other abstract collection implementations, the programme=
r does
 <i>not</i> have to provide an iterator implementation; the iterator and
 list iterator are implemented by this class, on top of the "random access"
 methods:
 <a href=3D"https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/util/AbstractLis=
t.html#get-int-"><code>get(int)</code></a>,
 <a href=3D"https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/util/AbstractLis=
t.html#set-int-E-"><code>set(int, E)</code></a>,
 <a href=3D"https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/util/AbstractLis=
t.html#add-int-E-"><code>add(int, E)</code></a> and
 <a href=3D"https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/util/AbstractLis=
t.html#remove-int-"><code>remove(int)</code></a>.

 </p><p>The documentation for each non-abstract method in this class descri=
bes its
 implementation in detail.  Each of these methods may be overridden if the
 collection being implemented admits a more efficient implementation.

 </p><p>This class is a member of the
 <a href=3D"https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/technotes/guides/collecti=
ons/index.html">
 Java Collections Framework</a>.</p></div>
